WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.200
Hi, this is Robert Sheer with another edition of Sheer Intelligence, the podcast I've been

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.680
doing for KCRW. And I have a wonderful guest tonight, John

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:36.240
Kariaku. He was a intelligence operative for the CIA for 14

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:42.720
years from 1990 to 2004. After the World Trade Center attack, he

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.000
was involved in Pakistan and the capture of the third highest

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.680
ranking leader of the Al Qaeda. And he blew the whistle on

00:00:52.680 --> 00:01:01.880
torture in 207, an interview with ABC. And after that, while he was working for the Senate

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.720
Foreign Relations Committee, he ran into some trouble because of an interview he gave to

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.840
a reporter for the New York Times in which he was said to have revealed the name of another

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:19.200
agent to that reporter. It's ironic because that's the sort of thing that General Petraeus,

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.640
who is head of the CIA did 10 times in books

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.560
that he turned over to his mistress, who

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.680
was writing a book about him.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.880
And he got no such penalty.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.720
My guest, John Carriacou, served over two years in prison.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.280
And what I want to ask you about,

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.360
because you know a lot about the so-called war on terrorism.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.480
And you know about what works and what doesn't work.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.880
And one of the first things that came up here

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.640
is that a number of high ranking officials

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.600
the head of the FBI, the head of the CIA,

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.840
and the former head of national security,

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.480
all jumped up before the TV cameras and say,

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.560
you see, this is why Apple and Google are wrong

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.560
to have encryption.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.840
This is why we can't have a restraints.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.840
This is why Edward Snowden is weakening us

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.000
and with his revelations and so forth.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.920
What was your immediate response to that?

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.080
Oh, it was first, a feeling of disgust.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.000
And second, I wanted to shout to the nearest person

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.640
in proximity to me what nonsense this was.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:28.640
This is exactly what our senior government officials do.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.640
Every time they're embarrassed by a revelation

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.860
is that they try to pass the blame on to somebody else.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:39.000
Ed Snowden is a very easy scapegoat.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.720
Google and Apple aren't going to defend themselves

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.280
publicly, the truth of the matter is

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:51.200
We can certainly fight against terrorism and fight terrorist groups around the world without

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.320
having to give up our own civil liberties.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.600
They're not mutually exclusive.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:03.080
And the government, whether it's a Republican president or a Democratic one, makes no difference.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.480
They both want us to give up our civil liberties.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.000
That's unnecessary.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.600
It's anti-constitutional.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.280
And I think Americans, all Americans, should stand up and oppose it.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.760
Yeah, the irony, of course, is because it could be Barack Obama, it could be George W. Bush,

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:23.040
they all claim they don't really want us to give up our civil liberties, they say it's a necessity.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.960
And one of the things that happened right away, and with an amount of hours of the Paris tragedy,

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:34.400
it was revealed that in fact the terrorists in this case had used unencrypted messaging.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.400
Exactly. That they were known to the authorities, that there was no mystery to it.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.920
They had telegraphed what they were going to do. Now you had worked

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:48.400
as a key. Why don't you give some of your background? Yeah, I served multiple tours overseas for the CIA

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:56.000
in Bahrain, in Athens, in Pakistan, throughout the Persian Gulf. I have a degree in Middle Eastern

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:05.520
studies. I speak Arabic. I spent virtually my entire adult life living in or working on the

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:11.280
Middle East. Even after I left the CIA, I joined the senior staff of the Senate Foreign Relations

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.400
committee. I was the senior investigator for the committee and I was the intelligence advisor to

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:21.920
its then chairman, Senator John Kerry of Massachusetts, who of course is now the Secretary of State.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:27.360
So I like to think that I know what I'm talking about as well. I was assigned at the CIA to the

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:32.960
Counterterrorism Center working specifically on terrorism issues and more specifically on

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:38.720
al-Qaeda and Sunni extremist groups. So again, I like to think I know what I'm talking about.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.360
In each case, we could have found these people. Some of them had been arrested, as in the case of

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:52.240
the Boston Marathon. The fellows now in the Paris bombing had announced on the internet publicly

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:57.520
and unequipped messages what they were about and so forth. You were one of the people out there

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.720
in those U.S. embassies out there in the field trying to recruit spies. And in fact, that's

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:08.320
how you were instrumental in the capture of the third most important member of Al-Qaeda.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.640
Can you take us through that story? What were you, what were you doing and how did you do it?

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.400
Immediately after the September 11th attacks, I volunteered to go to Afghanistan in any capacity

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:24.480
that the CIA wanted me. Four months passed before I was able to go overseas just because

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:30.720
my skill set was not one that was important in those really early days after the attacks.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.720
So finally in January of 2002, I was sent to Pakistan as the chief of counterterrorism

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:43.120
operations there. I had been in country for just a couple of weeks when we received word that Abu

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.360
Zubaydah was somewhere in the country. Now, we believed at the time that Abu Zubaydah was the

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:53.680
number three in al-Qaeda. I think that's probably not true in retrospect, but Abu Zubaydah was a

00:05:53.680 --> 00:06:00.080
very important facilitator and logistician for al-Qaeda. So if you're an al-Qaeda fighter

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:06.160
and you need a false passport, a credit card, a disguise, a safe house, a ticket home,

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:12.640
Abu Zubaydah is the person that you would go to for that. We were able to track Abu Zubaydah over

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:18.880
the course of about six weeks, and we were able to narrow down his location to one of 14

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:25.600
separate sites around Faisalabad, Pakistan, and a couple of sites around Lahore, Pakistan,

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:31.280
although it was less likely that he was in Lahore. We finally conducted simultaneous raids on all

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:39.840
14 of these sites at two o'clock in the morning on March 22, 2002. And sure enough, we found Abu

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:47.200
Zubaydah and more than four dozen other al-Qaeda fighters in these sites. So, Abu Zubaydah was

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:53.840
severely wounded during the capture. He was shot by a Pakistani policeman in the thigh,

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:59.760
the groin in the stomach with an AK-47, we were able to patch him up or have him

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:05.640
patched up and I sat with him for the next 56 hours before we put him on a

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:12.160
plane and sent him off to what it turned out was a secret prison in a third

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:18.040
country. You're doing what you said the CIA's function is to do and you've

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.800
written a book about it called The Reluctant Spy My Secret Life and the

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:29.160
CIA's were on terror and actually I was amazed at that this book which does discuss this escapade

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:33.220
Was actually approved for publication by the CIA. That was a fight

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:36.180
I should tell you it took me nine months to write this book and

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.800
22 months to get it cleared and the CIA ended up taking 90 pages out of it

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:46.600
So it was quite a battle. So I was gonna say where did you come from?

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:52.040
What led you to be a CIA agent and what led you now to be a whistleblower?

00:07:52.040 --> 00:08:01.200
Well, it's actually kind of a funny sort of romantic story in that it can't happen today.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:06.200
I was in graduate school at George Washington University and I was taking a class called

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:09.800
the Psychology of Leadership, a fantastic class.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:18.160
And so my professor tasked us with writing a psychological profile of our bosses.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.360
So I was working at the United Food and Commercial Workers Union at the time, and I worked for

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:26.640
a man who I was actually physically afraid of.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:32.760
I saw these outbursts of temper, even violence that frightened me.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:37.520
So I wrote a paper about him in which I concluded with supporting evidence that

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:44.440
he was a sociopath and that he had psychopathic tendencies. And I backed this up with evidence.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:49.480
So I handed in the paper. A week later I got the paper back and the professor had given

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:54.200
me an A and he wrote in the margin, I've never done this before but I urge you to

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:59.640
quit this job. In fact, I had quit the week earlier. And then he wrote, please see

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:05.640
me after class. So I went to see him after class and he closed the door of his office

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:11.800
and he said, look, I'm actually undercover as a professor here. I'm a senior CIA officer

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:17.400
acting as a spotter looking for grad students who I think would fit into the CIA's culture.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:19.480
Would you be interested in working for the CIA?

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:24.440
This is a scene out of a movie. This really happened.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:28.760
It really happened. And to tell you the truth, I had never really given any thought

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:33.800
to working for the CIA, but graduation was upon me. I was getting married just a week or

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:37.960
two after graduation. I had no job, no prospects for a job really.

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:42.520
This is graduation from grad school. And so I said, sure, I'd be interested in working

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:49.000
for the CIA. I went through the tests. He facilitated that. And then I ended up apparently doing

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:54.440
very well on that psychological test. So they called me and said, we've scheduled

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:59.240
the second battery of tests. We want you to go to this utterly nondescript building

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.240
in Vienna, Virginia.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.640
and you're going to take some tests there.

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:06.760
So I went out to this building and I was met there

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:11.040
by a secretary who took me into a small room

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.000
and there was a table and at the table were a psychiatrist,

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.000
a psychologist and an anthropologist.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.900
And there was a seat for me on the other side of the table.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:23.760
So I sat down, introduced myself, hello, how are you?

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:25.920
And the psychiatrist said to me,

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.040
describe your relationship with your mother.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:34.040
And I said, well, she's a good mother, I love her,

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:35.800
she loves me.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:37.640
And then we went on to the next question,

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.720
was your father the disciplinarian of the family?

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:42.960
And I said, not really, I think, you know,

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:46.320
my dad's a big strong guy, he was a big strong guy.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:49.200
I think he was probably afraid that he would hurt us,

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:51.000
so he never really disciplined us.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:53.520
He'd give us a stern look over his glasses,

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:56.440
which always frightened us as children.

00:10:56.440 --> 00:11:00.360
And then they said, and I remember this very clearly,

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.960
have you ever betrayed a friendship?

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:05.960
And I said, oh my gosh, I don't think so.

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:07.040
I hope not.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:08.680
I said, let me think about it for a minute.

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:10.800
And they said, no, no, that's the answer

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:11.880
we were looking for.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:14.840
And they all look at each other and they nod and nod.

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:18.800
And they said, okay, we're gonna need some hair,

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:21.800
blood and urine and you're free to go.

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:26.240
So I gave them some hair, blood, and urine, and I left.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:29.000
And I got home and my wife said, how'd you do?

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.160
I said, I have no idea.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:33.080
No idea whatsoever.

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.800
Then I got a call saying, hey, you aced those tests.

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:37.880
We'd like to see you at headquarters.

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:39.400
And at headquarters, I was interviewed

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.680
by several different offices, both in operations

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:42.760
and intelligence.

00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:44.880
And in fact, I ended up getting one job offer

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:48.120
and it was from the office that was founded

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:51.480
by my grad school professor.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:54.640
So I went into the office of leadership analysis

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:59.640
as an Iraq analyst in the first week of January, 1990.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:03.520
So you arrived there at the CIA

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:08.200
and they teach you Arabic and you're learning about Iraq.

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:11.120
Here we had been allies or a supporter.

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:13.960
We have this famous photo of Donald Rumsfeld

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:15.040
and Saddam Hussein together.

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:16.320
That's right, shaking hands.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:19.680
Yeah, and we were, we being the United States,

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:24.560
I didn't particularly vote for these guys who made the decision, but we were on the side

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:26.360
of Saddam Hussein against Iran.

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:33.120
In fact, we were providing tactical intelligence to Saddam Hussein in order to fight the Iranians.

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:40.640
We were providing not just defense intelligence, but overhead satellite imagery to help him

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:42.000
fight the Iranians.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:47.720
So it was actually a much closer intelligence and military cooperation relationship than

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:50.200
I think most Americans realized.

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:51.880
You're listening to Sheer Intelligence.

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:55.880
I'm speaking with former CIA agent John Kiriaco.

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:59.960
I want to get into the whole question of what has kept secret, what has classified, what

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:03.160
do we have a right to know, which is after all what we began with.

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:06.600
There's this terrible tragedy in Paris.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:11.360
It's once again being used as an excuse for tightening classification, for greater

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:14.400
surveillance, for invading the rights of Americans.

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:19.240
And what we lose with classification is what really happened in history, so we can't learn

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:20.240
the lessons.

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:26.200
How did Iran, one of the great centers of human history and development and culture, end up

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:28.160
being a center of madness?

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:31.520
And the U.S. and Brits have a real role in it.

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:35.760
So I'm taking you back now, you're a young guy in the CIA.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:42.680
And to what degree in that culture, was there any questioning, any thinking?

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:45.480
Absolutely none.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:49.880
Those experiences were seen as historical

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:51.780
and they had nothing to do with us.

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:54.080
They had nothing to do with the present.

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:55.720
Maybe mistakes were made.

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:58.980
We learned from those mistakes and we move on.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:03.760
I remember probing some senior officers

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:04.760
and asking questions.

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:06.560
Well, what was it like back then?

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:09.840
What was the decision making process like?

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:14.760
and people just not even being able to answer me

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:16.380
because they had never asked.

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:21.840
So I concluded on my own that we did not learn

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:25.520
from our mistakes because we never even discussed our mistakes.

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:28.720
What went wrong in your career or went right

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:31.600
depending on one's moral perspective

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:35.080
is that at first you were drinking the Kool-Aid.

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:36.120
Oh, sure.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:38.720
I was very proud to be a CIA officer.

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:42.680
You know, I always maintained, and I've said this publicly many times, the Republican

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:48.240
party and the conservative movement in this country do not have a monopoly on patriotism.

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:54.740
And so, you know, I was always a proud, progressive Democrat, and at the same time, proud to be

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:56.240
a CIA officer.

00:14:56.240 --> 00:15:00.680
There seemed to be a division between people who went into the CIA to do analysis and

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:06.680
thought and so forth, and the people out there doing the interrogating and the

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:13.100
special ops and all that and you were on that other side so you were I wasn't

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:17.400
usual you know if I were writing the script for the movie about you I mean

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:23.000
there you are in Pakistan and you are tracking the number three guy in Al-Qaeda

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:30.560
and and this guy gets captured right you basically organized yes right the

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:35.160
raid okay you've got this guy and instead of doing what they did with

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:40.080
bin Laden, you're trying to keep him alive. Yes. Right. Well, you're getting him a glass

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:46.120
of water. You're right. Yeah. Now, you personally did not witness torture. No, I did not. Abu

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:53.280
Zubaydah was tortured a couple of months after his arrival at the secret, more than a couple.

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:59.000
Actually, we captured him in late March 2002. The torture didn't begin until right around

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:04.600
the 1st of August. And I say right around because the White House authorized the torture

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:08.900
to begin on the 1st of August, but there's some evidence that he was tortured before

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:13.340
August in anticipation of the approval coming through.

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:15.500
So the torture began right around the 1st of August.

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:24.080
Now he had been in FBI custody really since right around the 1st of April, 2002.

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:29.540
And the FBI agent that was interrogating him, Ali Sufan, was actually doing a masterful

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:37.860
job at rapport building and at engaging him in a conversation to which he responded.

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:43.460
For example, Ali just sitting across the table from him and carrying on a conversation was

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:50.380
able to get Abu Zubaydah to identify a very mysterious character whom we had been hearing

00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:52.900
about, Muhtar.

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:57.660
For many years, we had first heard about Muhtar in the mid-1990s in relation to

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:04.460
the Bojinka Affair where this group called Al Qaeda that we had never heard of before

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:11.780
was planning to hijack six Boeing 747s, leaving Manila and crashing them into the sea.

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:14.820
So we knew that there was this bad man out there named Muhtar.

00:17:14.820 --> 00:17:16.140
We didn't know who that was.

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:20.480
It was Abu Zubaydah who said that Muhtar is Khaled Sheikh Mohammed.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:23.220
We had never heard of Khaled Sheikh Mohammed before.

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:27.660
And it turned out that it was Khaled Sheikh Mohammed who was the mastermind of the 9-11

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:28.660
attacks.

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:31.720
Again, all we had was this nom de guerre.

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:33.300
We knew this was a bad guy.

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:36.300
We had no idea who he was or where to find him.

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:41.340
And it was Ali Sufan who was able to draw that information out of Abu Zubaydah by

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:45.460
treating him with respect and by engaging him in a conversation.

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:51.860
The 9-11 Commission report, which is fascinating to reading, there's a narrative of how

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:58.260
this whole thing happened and who are these people and you know so forth. There's a disclaimer in

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:04.020
there where they say that the 9-11 commission people who after all had the highest level of

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:08.420
security clearance picked by the president of the United States and people of best experience

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:14.980
were not allowed they said we were not allowed to directly interview the key witnesses.

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:20.340
True. And nor were we allowed to actually interview the people who interviewed them.

00:18:20.340 --> 00:18:21.340
That's also true.

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:24.180
We put questions to them, so we really don't know.

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:27.020
And our analysis is limited by that fact.

00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:31.900
Well, the most interesting of these key witnesses was Khalid Sheikh Mohammed.

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:35.420
And what I have never been able to figure out, and I've read, I think, everything that's

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:41.580
out there about him, is how did this guy go from being a student in North Carolina

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:46.420
in a Christian school first, and then some sort of engineering school with, and there

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:51.420
There was no indication that he hated Americans or Christians or Jews.

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:55.420
No, in fact, he lived with an American family, like as an exchange student.

00:18:55.420 --> 00:19:00.420
And they said that he was a perfectly lovely guy while he was here in college.

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:05.420
So when we try to think about what makes a terrorist, what causes these movements,

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:11.420
and so forth, it's very difficult to get any sense at all of what drives them.

00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:17.780
And in the case of Khalid Sheikh Mohammed, you know, what caused this guy to go there?

00:19:17.780 --> 00:19:21.500
Was there any ever discussion about who are these people?

00:19:21.500 --> 00:19:24.860
Yeah, we tried as best we could to look into their backgrounds.

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:27.940
We found some very interesting things, like the fact that Khalid

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:31.100
Sheikh Mohammed had gone to school in North Carolina to a Christian college,

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:38.060
like I was a beta lived in Paris, sort of as a bohemian.

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:47.840
I mean we even found photos of Osama bin Laden in Stockholm wearing shorts and a sleeveless

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:55.040
t-shirt on tour with his brothers and sisters, 10 or 12 of his brothers and sisters.

00:19:55.040 --> 00:20:00.000
But we never really found, maybe it's because we didn't look so hard.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:07.100
But we never really found anything that allowed us to truly understand the genesis of this

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:08.600
transformation.

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:13.980
The $64 question, really, is, is there a blowback?

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:17.100
Are there policies that give rise to this?

00:20:17.100 --> 00:20:18.980
What causes people to become terrorists?

00:20:18.980 --> 00:20:23.740
It comes up in the Paris attack, and after all, this was not done by Syrian refugees.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:26.940
It was done by French nationals, right?

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:29.660
So what causes this?

00:20:29.660 --> 00:20:39.680
So much of what we do as part of our own foreign policy or national security policy creates,

00:20:39.680 --> 00:20:45.280
I believe, creates terrorists, for example, drone attacks.

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:54.760
We kill so many innocent civilians with the use of drones that we radicalize populations

00:20:54.760 --> 00:20:58.960
that would otherwise never have had a problem with us.

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:06.800
If you launch a drone attack on a suspected terrorist, okay, fine, you're going to kill

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:07.800
the terrorist.

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:12.920
But if you fire the drone while he's sitting at a cafe, let's say, which happens all the

00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:18.920
time, and you kill everybody at the cafe, which also happens, or you think you see

00:21:18.920 --> 00:21:23.800
somebody at a wedding and you drone the wedding, which has happened more times than I can

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:26.320
even count.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:32.440
You have successfully then radicalized all the surviving members of that family, people

00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:38.040
who otherwise would never have had a problem with the United States, and they're going

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:40.240
to seek vengeance.

00:21:40.240 --> 00:21:46.680
Was this true with the person that you were with when he was captured?

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:48.840
I don't think so.

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:55.040
He was a, for lack of a better phrase, I was a beta was a lost soul.

00:21:55.040 --> 00:22:05.700
I found him to be extraordinarily bright, not even really, not even really a true believer,

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:13.060
but I think he had, he had assessed his life and he had come to a conclusion that he was

00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:17.020
going nowhere and it's because he was Palestinian.

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:21.180
You know, he didn't think he would ever see a free Palestine and he just could think

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:31.740
of no other way to sort of fight for the cause other than to ally himself with Al Qaeda. Now,

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:37.000
he had never joined Al Qaeda. He had never pledged fealty to Osama bin Laden. But he was

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:46.500
certainly acting as Al Qaeda's, like I said, logistician or, you know, go to, what's

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:52.820
the word just go to guy for things like passports and safe houses and such, um, but he told me

00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:59.660
very directly that he was opposed to the September 11th attacks.

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:03.500
He supported the idea of an attack, but he wanted to attack Israel.

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:10.380
He said to me very plainly, all I ever wanted to do was to kill Jews is what he said.

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:14.660
But he said that bin Laden had overruled him and bin Laden had insisted that the

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:20.980
attack be against the United States. When you had him in custody, you were not engaging

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:25.780
in torture. No, no, certainly not. And you were making the point earlier that the FBI

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:30.300
was actually being quite effective in talking to him. Believe me, it kills me to compliment

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:34.100
the FBI, but they were actually quite good. Well, I gather from most of the accounts

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:39.820
it's the FBI that objected to the torture. Yes, not only did the FBI object to the

00:23:39.820 --> 00:23:45.900
torture. But as soon as it became apparent to Director Mueller that the CIA was going to begin

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:52.220
torturing prisoners, he ordered every single FBI agent out of the country. He said he didn't even

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:59.180
want his people in the same country where this was taking place. Ali Sufan reported when he

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:04.060
left the country and went back to FBI headquarters, he actually did engage with the

00:24:04.060 --> 00:24:10.980
Inspector General and reported what he had seen as a war crime and was told, well, you

00:24:10.980 --> 00:24:14.460
know, this has been approved at higher levels than ours.

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:16.980
So how do we know what he was able to find?

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:17.980
Has that been published?

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:18.980
Yeah.

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:23.180
He testified before the, I think it was the Senate Armed Services Committee back

00:24:23.180 --> 00:24:25.500
in 2008.

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:32.780
And even though the CIA forced heavy redactions on his testimony, much of it made

00:24:32.780 --> 00:24:35.060
its way into the public record.

00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:40.340
And so he was actually able to get the needed results without torture.

00:24:40.340 --> 00:24:41.340
Absolutely.

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:46.900
In fact, he was the only one who was able to get the results.

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:49.180
Torture or no torture.

00:24:49.180 --> 00:24:51.220
Now you've changed your position on that, I notice.

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:52.220
I have.

00:24:52.220 --> 00:24:56.300
You know, I tried, in 2007, Brian Ross of ABC News called me and said that he had

00:24:56.300 --> 00:24:58.980
a source who said that I had tortured Abuzabeta.

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:00.740
I said that was absolutely untrue.

00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:02.700
I had never tortured Abuzabeta.

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:05.100
or any other prisoner, I was the only person

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:06.740
who was kind to Abu Zubedah.

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:10.220
And he said, well, you're welcome to come on the show

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:11.580
and defend yourself.

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:13.780
So I said, I'd think about it.

00:25:13.780 --> 00:25:16.840
In the meantime, President Bush gave a press conference

00:25:16.840 --> 00:25:18.640
in which he looked directly into the camera

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:21.340
and said, we do not torture.

00:25:21.340 --> 00:25:23.280
And I knew that that was a lie.

00:25:24.340 --> 00:25:28.060
And a couple of days later, he told another reporter.

00:25:28.060 --> 00:25:28.900
Can I just interrupt?

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:30.060
How did you know it was a lie?

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:32.400
Because I knew we were torturing prisoners.

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:37.440
I was, in headquarters, I was the executive assistant to the deputy director for operations.

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:42.280
I was seeing all the cable traffic coming back, not just saying we're torturing, but

00:25:42.280 --> 00:25:49.480
saying in very, very clear detail, this is exactly what we're doing to torture these

00:25:49.480 --> 00:25:51.640
prisoners.

00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:52.640
So a couple of days later.

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:56.200
So all of the high command in the CIA would have known that.

00:25:56.200 --> 00:25:58.280
It was their idea.

00:25:58.280 --> 00:26:01.920
They came up with it.

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:03.640
were the names.

00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:08.080
Oh, we're talking about George Tenet and John McLaughlin

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:13.080
and Jose Rodriguez, several who are still undercover.

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:18.040
But John Rizzo, I mean, the fix was in.

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:21.760
George Tenet who wrote a whole book sort of justifying.

00:26:21.760 --> 00:26:22.600
Certainly.

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:24.280
This is his legacy.

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:25.720
He's gonna spend the rest of his life

00:26:25.720 --> 00:26:27.880
trying to justify this.

00:26:27.880 --> 00:26:29.600
He'll never come clean.

00:26:29.600 --> 00:26:33.440
So I go on ABC and I had decided in the days

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:36.080
before the interview that no matter what he asked me,

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:38.280
I was going to tell the truth.

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:40.920
And so I said three things that night

00:26:40.920 --> 00:26:43.520
that utterly changed the course of my life.

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:45.920
I said, we were torturing prisoners.

00:26:45.920 --> 00:26:49.240
I said, torture was official US government policy.

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:52.040
And I said, the policy was approved by and signed

00:26:52.040 --> 00:26:54.240
by the president himself.

00:26:54.240 --> 00:26:57.400
And within 24 hours, the FBI began investigating me.

00:26:57.400 --> 00:27:01.520
Now interestingly enough, a year later at the very end

00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:06.000
of the Bush administration, the Justice Department

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:08.160
decided that I had not committed a crime

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:10.480
and they closed the investigation.

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:14.440
Three months later, the new Attorney General, Eric Holder,

00:27:14.440 --> 00:27:17.560
ordered that the investigation be reopened.

00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:20.240
And so they reopened it and they

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:24.680
were able to manufacture five felony charges against me.

00:27:24.680 --> 00:27:29.200
So, you know, I make a point about the Espionage Act all the time, between the time that the

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:35.080
Espionage Act was written in 1917 until Barack Obama's election, three people in American

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:41.240
history were charged with Espionage for passing classified information to the press, three

00:27:41.240 --> 00:27:44.120
people in American history.

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:50.240
During the Obama administration, 10 people have been charged with Espionage for passing

00:27:50.240 --> 00:27:51.880
information to the press.

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:57.240
I'm talking to John Carriaco who blew the whistle on torture in the CIA.

00:27:57.240 --> 00:28:02.600
You mentioned the official that really went after you.

00:28:02.600 --> 00:28:05.280
Was that some Dick Cheney type right?

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:06.280
No.

00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:07.280
It wasn't a neocon nut.

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:08.280
It's a liberal.

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:09.280
Yeah.

00:28:09.280 --> 00:28:10.280
Liberal.

00:28:10.280 --> 00:28:11.280
That's right.

00:28:11.280 --> 00:28:15.160
A guy named Eric Holder who I actually met, believe it or not, not far from here at

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:18.840
a dinner at Barbara Streisand's house.

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:21.280
And Barbara Streisand, a good person.

00:28:21.280 --> 00:28:26.200
She had a small dinner party, and Eric Holder was there when Obama was first running.

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:31.480
And he presented as an incredibly enlightened, pro-civil liberties, decent fellow.

00:28:31.480 --> 00:28:32.480
Right.

00:28:32.480 --> 00:28:37.240
Yeah, the bankers will tell you that he's quite enlightened.

00:28:37.240 --> 00:28:39.680
And yet, because he didn't go after them.

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:40.680
Right.

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:41.680
Yeah.

00:28:41.680 --> 00:28:42.680
But he went after you.

00:28:42.680 --> 00:28:43.680
Yeah.

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:46.520
The guy who sat next to me at this dinner and was so reasonable and agreed with

00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:52.400
me on everything. He actually destroyed or tried to destroy your life.

00:28:52.400 --> 00:28:58.440
Yeah, I've not recovered. I'm not sure that I ever will. I'll tell you exactly the basis

00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:03.760
of these three espionage charges. I worked with an analyst by the name of Deuce Martinez

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:11.240
who had never, ever been undercover, ever. And Deuce Martinez resigned from the CIA about

00:29:11.240 --> 00:29:16.020
a year after I did. We were friends and he was excited to tell me about his

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:21.480
onward position. So we had lunch together and he gave me his new business card and

00:29:21.480 --> 00:29:26.220
it said, Deuce Martinez, Mitchell and Jessen LLC. Now Mitchell and Jessen were

00:29:26.220 --> 00:29:30.180
the creators, the architects of the torture program and I said to him, are

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:36.100
you crazy going to work for these monsters? And he said, no, no, they're

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:40.620
good guys, they're misunderstood. I said, you've got to be insane to

00:29:40.620 --> 00:29:44.780
resign from the agency and go to work for these guys of all people.

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:47.900
There wasn't one other job that you could have taken.

00:29:47.900 --> 00:29:49.060
These are the psychologists?

00:29:49.060 --> 00:29:51.740
The two psychologists.

00:29:51.740 --> 00:29:56.340
When I went on ABC News, he stopped speaking to me, which was fine.

00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:58.140
But I happen to have his business card.

00:29:58.140 --> 00:29:59.740
Scott Shane of the New York Times.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:01.040
called me and said,

00:30:01.040 --> 00:30:04.280
I'm working on a story about Deuce Martinez.

00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:05.920
Do you know how I can get in touch with him?

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:07.680
And I said, gee, I haven't talked to him in a long time.

00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:09.000
He stopped speaking to me.

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:09.840
But you know what?

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:11.320
I have his business card.

00:30:11.320 --> 00:30:12.760
So I scanned the business card

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:15.680
and I emailed it to Scott Shane.

00:30:15.680 --> 00:30:18.000
I was charged with espionage for that email.

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:20.520
Shane's article came out in the New York Times,

00:30:20.520 --> 00:30:22.320
a reporter for ABC News called and said,

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:24.440
hey, do you know how I can get in touch

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:25.600
with Deuce Martinez?

00:30:25.600 --> 00:30:27.000
I sent him the business card.

00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:29.600
I got a second espionage charge.

00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:34.000
And then the Justice Department made a big production

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:36.520
out of telling the judge that I had passed

00:30:36.520 --> 00:30:41.320
top secret code word information to the press.

00:30:41.320 --> 00:30:44.120
And that information was declassified

00:30:44.120 --> 00:30:46.840
solely for the purpose of prosecuting me.

00:30:46.840 --> 00:30:50.400
And that information was that after the September 11th

00:30:50.400 --> 00:30:53.400
attacks, the CIA was trying to kill

00:30:53.400 --> 00:30:55.400
or capture Osama bin Laden.

00:30:57.480 --> 00:30:58.360
Top secret.

00:30:59.600 --> 00:31:04.200
Now, the judge threw out those three espionage charges.

00:31:04.200 --> 00:31:10.120
I hadn't committed espionage, but this is what Holder's Justice Department does.

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:17.000
They heap multiple major felony charges on you, knowing that you're going to run out

00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:22.360
of money, that your friends and family will eventually turn against you, you're going

00:31:22.360 --> 00:31:27.600
to exhaust yourself, and eventually, you're going to take a plea just to make the

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:34.440
rest of it go away. Well, I ran up legal bills of well over a million dollars. I

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:41.480
still owe my attorneys $880,000 and we didn't even go to trial. If we were to

00:31:41.480 --> 00:31:45.040
go to trial, they told me we're looking at a three million dollar legal bill

00:31:45.040 --> 00:31:48.640
here. What do you want to do? Because if we lose, you're realistically going to

00:31:48.640 --> 00:31:53.720
get 12 to 18 years. The government's offering two and a half. So what do

00:31:53.720 --> 00:31:59.320
do you want to do? Well, I have five kids at home. So do I take the two and a half and

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:06.040
an $880,000 outstanding bill or do I roll the dice for 12 to 18 years and a $3 million

00:32:06.040 --> 00:32:10.800
legal bill? The decision's an easy one when you put it like that.

00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:18.400
So the good guy puts you in jail, destroys your family life, destroys your economic

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:26.440
situation. And you're in prison. We're not going to go into great detail. But you're in

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:29.640
prison. Did you have regrets?

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:35.720
Oh, no, no, no, no regrets at all. I would have done the whole thing again.

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:36.360
Why?

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:40.800
Because you can't have regrets. Otherwise you make yourself crazy. Now, the only thing

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:44.040
I would have done differently is I would have hired an attorney before going on

00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:52.760
ABC rather than after. But in retrospect, I'm on the right side of history on this issue.

00:32:52.760 --> 00:32:57.780
It's the government that's wrong on torture. You got out of prison, you were on democracy

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:04.640
now and then I interviewed you along with my producer, Josh Shear, we interviewed you.

00:33:04.640 --> 00:33:10.000
And you told me a story that wasn't the most sensational story of hardship, but

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:12.240
I was really taken aback by it.

00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:20.200
A former colleague of mine from both the CIA and the Senate Foreign Relations Committee

00:33:20.200 --> 00:33:25.640
came over to congratulate me on my release, and it was great seeing him.

00:33:25.640 --> 00:33:33.180
And he said that he was bringing a message from a former boss of mine, who really both

00:33:33.180 --> 00:33:35.240
of us were friends with.

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:39.760
But that boss had since assumed a very senior position in the administration.

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:48.940
So he said, I have a message from our friend, congratulations, he's really glad you're home,

00:33:48.940 --> 00:33:53.600
he hopes you understand that he can never speak to you again.

00:33:53.600 --> 00:33:59.760
And I said, yeah, sure, I understand, I think he's a coward, but I understand.

00:33:59.760 --> 00:34:07.360
We have lectured the whole world about civil liberties, integrity, totalitarianism and

00:34:07.360 --> 00:34:08.360
so forth.

00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:17.940
This is really a 1984 moment, it really is, and that this person in a high position would

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:21.200
just make you basically a non-person.

00:34:21.200 --> 00:34:22.200
That's what's happened.

00:34:22.200 --> 00:34:23.200
I'm a non-person.

00:34:23.200 --> 00:34:24.200
Yes.

00:34:24.200 --> 00:34:25.200
I've lost everything.

00:34:25.200 --> 00:34:29.520
They even took my pension, my federal pension from me, after 19 and a half years of proud

00:34:29.520 --> 00:34:33.200
federal service, I'm going to have to work until the day I die.

00:34:33.200 --> 00:34:35.800
And that's really part of the Justice Department plan.

00:34:35.800 --> 00:34:38.560
What do you mean it's the Justice Department plan?

00:34:38.560 --> 00:34:40.640
What does that really mean?

00:34:40.640 --> 00:34:42.640
What is an Eric Holder?

